\import{swarm-macros}

\title{Weeknotes for 25 September 2026 (week 38)}
\date{2026-09-28}
\tag{weeknote}

\put\transclude/numbered{false}

\subtree{
  \title{Celebrations and farewells}

  \p{This past week has been extremely full, and a rollercoaster of emotions. My wife attended a conference at the beginning of the week, so I was solo parenting our three kids Monday–Wednesday.  On Thursday my wife and I celebrated 23 years together—not our wedding anniversary (which we also celebrate), but the day we officially began dating.  On Friday afternoon I attended a farewell party for a dear colleague who is leaving suddenly and unexpectedly for a wonderful opportunity—I'm excited for her but very sad she is leaving. On Saturday morning, we hosted a bunch of 8–9 year olds for a Harry Potter-themed birthday party (featuring a fun [escape room/puzzle solving challenge](https://www.etsy.com/listing/1003638944/wizard-escape-room-game-adventure-party?ls=a&ga_order=most_relevant&ga_search_type=all&ga_view_type=gallery&ga_search_query=potter+escape+room&ref=sc_gallery-1-1&sr_prefetch=1&pf_from=market&dd=1&plkey=EuWZgBWIwFfXHghrvDUNMQeCZo6e\%3ALT1dd4b556090b6df20e994de15ae12e08c5a3b936)); then on Saturday afternoon we attended the funeral of a dear friend from church (at which I also played the piano).}

  \p{Needless to say, I did not get very much work done this week.  I dread having to claw my way out from under the pile of tasks and grading that has accumulated.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Piano corner}

  \p{I finally feel like I have Chopin's [Waltz in A♭ major, Op. 64 No. 3](00H6) satisfactorily under my fingers, which I started learning back in June and have worked off and on since then.  This one took a lot longer to learn than some of the others—it is not particularly demanding technically, but it seems to have a relatively high [Kolmogorov complexity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolmogorov_complexity).}

  \p{I have also polished up Debussy's [Clair de Lune](00NM), which I learned last year but hadn't played in a little while.  It is of course [quite famous](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suite_bergamasque), and I resisted learning it for a long time just because of some perverse nonconformist urge.  But there is a reason it is so popular: it is quite beautiful, and I finally decided that it's just one of those pieces any classical pianist should have in their repertoire.  Besides, although it is fairly accessible, and many people try to learn it due to its popularity, playing it well is a nontrivial challenge.  I'm fairly happy with how I play it at this point.}
}
